{"signature":{"version":"1.2","source":"NASA/JPL Horizons API"},"result":"*******************************************************************************\n Revised: Oct 07, 2026  Martian Moons eXploration (MMX) spacecraft / Mars  -239\n                          https://www.mmx.jaxa.jp/en/mission/\n\n MISSION LAUNCH DATE:                            MARS ARRIVAL DATE:\n  October 19, 2026 19:41:03 UTC                   August, 2027\n\n LAUNCH VEHICLE:\n  H3 (from Tanegashima Space Center, Japan)              \n\n Background:\n  MMX (Martian Moons eXploration) is a sample-return mission led by JAXA \n  (Japan Aerospace Exploration Agency) designed to explore Mars's moons, \n  Phobos and Deimos. \n\n  The project involves major contributions from NASA (providing the MEGANE \n  gamma-ray and neutron spectrometer), ESA, CNES, and DLR. \n\n  The spacecraft's goal is to clarify the origin of the Martian moons and \n  the evolution of the Mars system by collecting and returning at least \n  10 grams of surface material from Phobos to Earth. \n\n  The mission involves a five-year round-trip journey:\n\n   2026-Oct-19: Depart Earth\n   2027-Aug   : Arrive at Mars. Orbit Mars. Enter a quasi-satellite orbit\n                around Phobos to conduct detailed remote sensing prior to\n                landing and collecting a sample.\n   2030       : Depart Martian system\n   2031       : Return to Earth\n\n SPACECRAFT PHYSICAL PROPERTIES:\n  Length      : 9 meters \n  Height      : 5 meters\n  Launch mass : 4480 kg\n  Dry mass    : 2100 kg (s/c only)\n  Propellant  : 2380 kg\n  Idefix rover: 25 kg (detachable, operate on surface of Phobos for 100 days)\n\n  Three modules: \n   - propulsion module for transit (~1990 kg), \n   - exploration module for landing and sampling (~150 kg), \n   - return module for the journey back (~1350 kg).\n\n INSTRUMENTS:\n It carries 11 scientific instruments and a small rover named Idefix which \n was jointly developed by CNES and DLR, to support surface exploration.\n The Idefix rover carries 4 science instruments. \n\n SPACECRAFT TRAJECTORY: \n  Pre-launch planning trajectory from JAXA starts ~1h 18m after launch:\n\n Trajectory (JAXA)                                     Start          End\n -------------------------------------------------   -----------   -----------\n 20261019_20281231_MMX_V0.2                          2026-Oct-19   2028-Dec-31\n*******************************************************************************\n\n\n*******************************************************************************\nEphemeris / API_USER Fri Oct 16 08:56:48 2026 Pasadena, USA      / Horizons\n*******************************************************************************\nTarget body name: MMX (spacecraft) (-239)         {source: 20261019_20281231_MMX}\nCenter body name: Sun (10)                        {source: DE441}\nCenter-site name: BODY CENTER\n*******************************************************************************\nStart time      : A.D. 2027-Aug-29 04:00:30.0000 UT \nStop  time      : A.D. 2027-Aug-29 04:56:30.0000 UT \nStep-size       : 1 minutes\n*******************************************************************************\nCenter geodetic : 0.0, 0.0, 0.0                   {E-lon(deg),Lat(deg),Alt(km)}\nCenter cylindric: 0.0, 0.0, 0.0                   {E-lon(deg),Dxy(km),Dz(km)}\nCenter radii    : 695700.0, 695700.0, 695700.0 km {Equator_a, b, pole_c}\nOutput units    : KM-S\nCalendar mode   : Mixed Julian/Gregorian\nOutput type     : GEOMETRIC cartesian states\nOutput format   : 2 (position and velocity)\nEOP file        : eop.261015.p270111\nEOP coverage    : DATA-BASED 1962-JAN-20 TO 2026-OCT-15. PREDICTS-> 2027-JAN-10\nReference frame : ICRF\n*******************************************************************************\n            JDUT ,            Calendar Date (UT ),                      X,                      Y,                      Z,                     VX,                     VY,     